  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As part of a regional effort to strengthen compliance with international labour standards and address high rates of violence against women and girls&#x2014;particularly among unprotected domestic workers&#x2014;the ILO supported Tuvalu in February 2025 through an International Labour Standards workshop focused on the Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention, 1999 (No. 182). Government, employer, and worker representatives jointly drafted Tuvalu&#x2019;s first report under the Convention and began developing a national list of hazardous work prohibited for children. The ILO also conducted a gap analysis on the Forced Labour Convention, 1930 (No. 29), identifying areas for legal and policy alignment. Despite limited resources, Tuvalu has made steady progress, with the first report under Convention No. 182 expected by 1 September 2025&#x2014;marking a significant step toward improved reporting, stronger protections for vulnerable workers, and deeper engagement with the ILO supervisory system</narrative>
  </description>
